  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "UK jungle sleeve, working sampler",
    "overview": "The user-supplied 1990s UK jungle sleeve and graffiti idioms inform the approved original JUNGLIZER wordmark, including its J/I cleanup. Olive-black, ivory, and lime give the native instrument its atmosphere. Functional controls remain legible and compact around the source-to-player workflow.\n\nProduct truth lives in [the UI design brief](docs/UI-DESIGN-BRIEF.md). Implemented tokens and interactions live in [src/ui.rs](src/ui.rs); this document captures that implementation rather than the brief's earlier incumbent palette. The later user-supplied [Opus playing-editor reference](docs/comps/Opus/Editor%20%C2%B7%20playing,%20placement%20selected.png) and companion tokens image informed more efficient grouping. The user preferred our original logo. References guide grouping and source identity, not glossy material imitation: this remains flat native egui. The revised grouping received a ship disposition after review of all 13 rendered states, with no required design fixes. Screenshots are deterministic actual-egui fixtures, not Logic captures or human/DAW interaction verification.\n\nOutput is a far-right 66 px rotary control with an adjacent dB readout; the 808 root selector is 88 px wide.\n\nThin/Thicc uses a 280 px chunky crossfader with a recessed rail, ridged ivory cap, and Dry center marks.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Graffiti identity with restrained native controls.",
      "Source and player visible together at the default size.",
      "Break identity reinforced by numbers and state outlines."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Identity Rule",
        "body": "Break colors identify audio sources; selection, locks, and playback also need labels, outlines, or symbols.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Host Gesture Rule",
        "body": "Preserve parameter IDs and `PluginContext` paths: slider drags use `begin_edit` \u2192 `set_param` \u2192 `end_edit`; discrete keyboard/click edits use `automate`. Each UI frame synchronizes state and host-driven selections. Styling must not bypass host automation.",
        "section": "components"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** preserve the approved wordmark and the four numbered break identities.",
      "**Do** check ghost, selected, muted, locked, live, frozen, stopped, playing, dense, export, and missing-source states in actual egui renders.",
      "**Do** describe fixture screenshots separately from in-host verification."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** add ornamental dashboard cards or decorative hardware panels.",
      "**Don't** conflate Freeze with placement locks or conceal disabled-action explanations.",
      "**Don't** treat HTML previews or an HTML detector as validation of this native Rust UI."
    ]
  }
